  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "The mean score on a national mathematics contest is $\\overset{\u00af}{x}=80$ points with a standard deviation of $5$ points. What is the $z\\text{-score}$ that corresponds to a student's score of $65$ points?\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{\u2015}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=65$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{\u00af}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{65-80}{5} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
